1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,130
Ghana just launched
something called Marrify.

2
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:06,810
It's like a public platform where
you can search and verify marriages,

3
00:00:06,830 --> 00:00:10,060
divorces and licensed marriage officials.

4
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:13,770
It launched last month with
some Ministry of Gender,

5
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:16,960
and I was just reading about
it the other day on TechCabal.

6
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:21,780
And the way that it works is that it pulls
data from different government sources,

7
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,760
the Registrar-General's Department,
the courts, local assembly,

8
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:28,560
all of that. It covers all kinds of
marriages and all kinds of divorces.

9
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:31,680
It goes all the way back, like
records from all the way back.

10
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:35,100
And you can also check whether a church
or a pastor and a marriage venue is

11
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:37,410
actually licensed to conduct a marriage.

12
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:39,720
And I really love this when I
saw this, because as an African,

13
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:43,710
I'm thinking, wow, we're having
centralised data, finally, finally!

14
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:48,080
Now before I talk about drawbacks,
because I read TechCabal's article about

15
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:50,080
this, and there were
some drawbacks it cited.

16
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:52,730
I've been living in Estonia for about
a year now, and people always say,

17
00:00:52,750 --> 00:00:55,420
like, this place is the digital hub
of Europe or something like that.

18
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:57,600
And I'm not going to lie, it's so real.

19
00:00:57,620 --> 00:00:59,680
Everything here, everything
you want to do here is online.

20
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:01,010
I'm not exaggerating. Everything.

21
00:01:01,030 --> 00:01:03,750
Your health records,
everything links so well.

22
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:06,680
There is nothing you want to do
that you can't do online here.

23
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:09,270
You can get married online,
you can divorce online.

24
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:11,990
There's a link for everything
you want to do in Estonia.

25
00:01:12,030 --> 00:01:16,080
The data ecosystem that the
governments use, that they run here,

26
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:19,280
the way it syncs, I genuinely admire it.

27
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:22,030
And as someone coming from Nigeria,
it blew my mind when I got it,

28
00:01:22,050 --> 00:01:26,437
where everything just syncs. And I see
Ghana doing something in this direction…

29
00:01:26,457 --> 00:01:29,270
my first instinct is like,
yes, please, more of this,

30
00:01:29,290 --> 00:01:30,610
because it solves so many problems.

31
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:34,100
There's one reliable record instead of
like just pieces of paper everywhere,

32
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:36,260
instead of NIN and this and that.

33
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:40,000
And I think the main thing that even made
me record this video is the drawback

34
00:01:40,020 --> 00:01:43,950
that TechCabal raised, which is that
marriage and divorce records are sensitive

35
00:01:43,970 --> 00:01:48,160
private data. I don't know, like a
divorce record is a personal thing about

36
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:52,660
someone's life. And now that record is
searchable by anyone that has your name.

37
00:01:52,870 --> 00:01:55,320
And I'm saying this as
someone that likes open data.

38
00:01:55,340 --> 00:01:56,340
In Estonia,

39
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:58,080
you can search online and find businesses.

40
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:00,540
You can check how much a business,
like, you don't know anything about,

41
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:01,920
You can look up how much
they made in a year.

42
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:04,530
And that's fine, I think, because
if business is a public entity,

43
00:02:04,550 --> 00:02:07,340
that takes public money,
holds public accountability.

44
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:10,050
But maybe in the case of a divorce,
it's a little bit different.

45
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:11,980
So that really got me
thinking about the drawbacks,

46
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:13,000
of course.

47
00:02:13,050 --> 00:02:16,360
I don't really know, because
I love the idea of open data,

48
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:20,730
but I also really don't know how to balance
it well with personal data and privacy

49
00:02:20,750 --> 00:02:23,430
issues. And there's also the
consent part of the conversation,

50
00:02:23,950 --> 00:02:27,612
like the difference between a divorce
record sitting in a courthouse somewhere

51
00:02:27,632 --> 00:02:28,632
in 2003,

52
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:34,160
and somebody just being able to check it
on their phone in three seconds, right?

53
00:02:34,980 --> 00:02:38,540
And I was also then thinking
about data minimisation.

54
00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:42,120
So the idea of data minimisation is that
you expose the minimum amount of data

55
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:44,280
required to answer the
question being asked.

56
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:47,180
So if I ask the question, is this
person currently free to marry?

57
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:50,670
That's a yes or no answer. Answering
that question does not require showing

58
00:02:50,690 --> 00:02:53,660
when they got married, who they
married, when they divorced,

59
00:02:53,710 --> 00:02:57,790
which court granted it, right? I was also
thinking about how data minimisation

60
00:02:58,020 --> 00:03:01,950
can come to play here as well. Could
we minimise data to the point where we

61
00:03:01,970 --> 00:03:04,570
are not exposing much more
than it's been asked for?

62
00:03:04,620 --> 00:03:07,910
Like in the case of Marrify in
Ghana, for example, the question is,

63
00:03:07,930 --> 00:03:11,970
is this person married? So the answer
is simple, this person currently has an

64
00:03:11,990 --> 00:03:15,380
active marriage on record.
Nothing else, no name of spouse,

65
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:19,780
no dates, no history. But of course,
another person could request another thing.

66
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:21,480
And then I was thinking
about like access level.

67
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:25,970
Maybe perhaps an individual could access
a level of this data and maybe there

68
00:03:25,990 --> 00:03:28,680
are certain organizations or
institutions that can access more.

69
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:30,180
I was just thinking about all of it.

70
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:34,180
And again, I always say this,
that technology is not neutral.

71
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:37,400
It works, it takes the form of the
system or the place that you're using it

72
00:03:37,420 --> 00:03:40,760
in. For example, the way a piece of technology
works in Estonia will be completely

73
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:43,250
different from the way it's
working in a country in Africa,

74
00:03:43,270 --> 00:03:46,850
in Nigeria, in Ghana. In Estonia, a divorce
record could just be administrative,

75
00:03:46,950 --> 00:03:50,014
nobody cares. But in an African
context, to a large extent,

76
00:03:50,034 --> 00:03:52,880
divorce, especially for
women, is pretty stigmatised.

77
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:56,920
So it's very tricky when I think about
it, when I think about the drawbacks

78
00:03:56,940 --> 00:04:00,830
first, that this is something that I
myself think I need to explore further,

79
00:04:00,850 --> 00:04:04,480
the human side of this thing. It's not
just a privacy engineering conversation,

80
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:06,470
it's also a cultural design thing.

81
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:07,980
But I've just been
putting it side by side.

82
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,040
I really love the idea, but another
thing I find genuinely smart in Estonia

83
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:14,534
is the idea that your data being in the
system is also paired with you being

84
00:04:14,554 --> 00:04:15,880
able to see what's happening to it.

85
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:16,900
That transparency

86
00:04:17,070 --> 00:04:22,300
runs both ways. And I also think that
if we are creating open data in Africa,

87
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:24,610
we also need to ask the
question of reciprocity.

88
00:04:25,050 --> 00:04:27,680
Where is the reciprocity
for the average individual?

89
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:31,330
If someone looks up my marriage record,
do I get to see that someone looked

90
00:04:31,410 --> 00:04:34,620
it up? I really want African countries
building this kind of infrastructure,

91
00:04:34,690 --> 00:04:36,540
because I genuinely think
it also solves a problem.

92
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:40,200
Technology helps when it
comes to things like this.

93
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:43,320
But I also think that if we want to
build this kind of infrastructure,

94
00:04:43,700 --> 00:04:45,880
as people, we still need to probe
it a little bit and question it.

95
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:47,580
And that's what I've just
been doing in my head.

96
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:48,980
I really like the idea I read about.

97
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,880
It's super, super cool. I really love it.

98
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:55,080
But again, just thinking about
the drawbacks and everything.

99
00:04:55,100 --> 00:04:57,310
this is just what's been
running in my head. Yeah.
